# kes-round.allowed-errors — checked-in allowlist for assert_no_other_errors  (#1037/#1041)
#
# One extended-regex per line; blank lines and `#` comments are ignored
# (lib/expect-log-errors.sh's assert_no_other_errors). Every entry here is an
# ERROR-class line (per $LOG_ERROR_PATTERN in
# .claude/skills/devnet-validate/scripts/lib/log-level-counts.sh) that
# kes-round.sh EXPECTS on dugite-bp's log because the round deliberately
# provoked it. Each entry here has a matching expect_log_errors call in
# kes-round.sh — an allowlist entry without one would silently widen the net
# for no reason.

# Step 2 (#1037): once dugite-bp's KES key evolves past maxKESEvolutions,
# every subsequent VRF-elected slot bails out of forge_block() with this
# exact message (crates/dugite-node/src/forge.rs:344-351), logged at ERROR by
# the TraceForgeStateUpdateError wrapper around the forge_block() call site
# (crates/dugite-node/src/node/mod.rs, ~line 10560-10570). This is the fault
# under test — see the matching expect_log_errors in step 2. Left un-anchored
# at the tail (no "> max ..." suffix) so a future change to
# slotsPerKESPeriod/maxKESEvolutions in the genesis overlay doesn't also need
# an edit here.
TraceForgeStateUpdateError: block forging failed: KES key expired

# Steps 3-5 each SIGTERM dugite-bp and relaunch it via
# restart-dugite-bp.sh with new KES/opcert files. dugite-bp's own graceful-
# shutdown path logs this at ERROR if it exceeds its internal 30s
# flush/persist/quiesce budget before forcing exit
# (crates/dugite-node/src/node/mod.rs:6190). kes-round.sh's own
# stop_dugite_bp() already waits up to 40s for the process to die before
# proceeding, so this should not fire in the common case — allowlisted
# defensively for a slow CI runner, since a restart that is merely SLOW is
# not the defect this round exists to catch (the round's own post-restart
# forging/adoption checks are the load-bearing assertions, not this line).
Graceful shutdown \(flush/persist/quiesce\) timed out after 30s, forcing exit
